WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.340
SCENES THAT ALMOST CAUSED THE ACTORS TO QUIT THEIR JOBS

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.920
It can seem like Hollywood is all glitz and
glamour, but a lot of real, hard work behind

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:15.340
the scenes goes into making movies. And that
work can test a person's limits not just as

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.490
an actor but as a human being.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:21.670
MICHELLE RODRIGUEZ IN THE FAST AND THE FURIOUS

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:26.000
During production on the first Fast and Furious
movie, Michelle Rodriguez actually considered

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.289
walking away over concerns with the way her
character, Letty Ortiz, was written. Rodriguez

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:36.210
took issue with the way the script had Letty
betray her true love Dom, and fall for undercover

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:41.390
cop Brian. The actress said the betrayal made
no sense for a woman who'd prefer an alpha

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.840
male to a pretty boy. Refusing to betray her
vision of the character, Rodriguez put her

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:52.210
foot down, telling producers she'd quit the
movie if she had to. Fortunately for fans,

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.780
the storyline was tweaked to Rodriguez's liking,
and the rest is blockbuster history.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.059
LAUREN COHAN IN THE WALKING DEAD

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:05.070
The Walking Dead nearly suffered a devastating
loss in season three. Lauren Cohan, who plays

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:10.459
Maggie, contemplated quitting over a particularly
stressful scene. The script had her character

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:15.679
perform an emergency c-section and she was
worried it would be too intense. But after

00:01:15.679 --> 00:01:20.119
further consideration, that's exactly why
she decided to stay. Recognizing that The

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:25.139
Walking Dead is a show about people pushed
to grueling extremes, she drew on her discomfort

00:01:25.139 --> 00:01:29.259
to deliver an affectingly honest performance.

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:32.319
JIM CARREY IN HOW THE GRINCH STOLE CHRISTMAS

00:01:32.319 --> 00:01:36.279
Jim Carrey nabbed the role of the Grinch after
making it through an audition process that

00:01:36.279 --> 00:01:41.490
included earning the approval of Dr. Seuss'
widow. But that obstacle was minor compared

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.169
with what he faced on the set. He had a hard
time in the makeup chair, where he spent hours

00:01:46.169 --> 00:01:50.999
each day during the lengthy shoot. Worse,
he had to wear uncomfortable contact lenses

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:56.099
that proved so painful that the studio brought
in a military torture adviser to help Carrey

00:01:56.099 --> 00:02:01.899
cope. Those techniques got him through filming
without quitting, but just barely. In fact,

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:05.809
some shots of the Grinch's eyes had to be
digitally fixed in post-production because

00:02:05.809 --> 00:02:09.000
Carrey couldn't bear to wear the contacts.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.200
IAN MCKELLEN IN THE HOBBIT: AN UNEXPECTED
JOURNEY

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.439
It's impossible to imagine Gandalf being played
by anyone other than Sir Ian McKellen, who

00:02:16.439 --> 00:02:21.000
put his stamp on the character in all six
Lord of the Rings and Hobbit films. But at

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.890
one point during The Hobbit: An Unexpected
Journey, director Peter Jackson might have

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.709
needed a different actor for the part. The
problem? McKellen was acting almost exclusively

00:02:29.709 --> 00:02:33.750
to a green screen, owing to the fact that
his character towered over the hobbits, and

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.430
their interactions had to be stitched together
digitally in post-production. The frustration

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.780
of acting alone eventually got to McKellen,
who broke down on the set at one point. Luckily,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.310
Jackson did a little emergency outreach to
convince him to stick around.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.250
JESSICA ALBA IN FANTASTIC FOUR: RISE OF THE
SILVER SURFER

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.379
You think you hated Fox's first couple of
Fantastic Four movies? Try being Jessica Alba,

00:02:57.379 --> 00:03:02.200
whose experiences playing Sue "Invisible Woman"
Storm nearly made her walk away from acting

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.510
altogether. Alba's frustrations stemmed from
working with director Tim Story. She later

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.920
claimed he had absurd demands, like telling
her to keep her facial expression flat during

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.780
a crying scene so she'd stay "pretty." After
2007's Rise of the Silver Surfer, she realized

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.040
she didn't care about the movie business anymore.
But things have still worked out pretty well

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:28.370
for her: she went on to co-found the Honest
Company, currently valued around $2 billion.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.829
BRAD PITT IN INTERVIEW WITH THE VAMPIRE

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:35.689
Interview with the Vampire united Tom Cruise
and Brad Pitt in an adaptation of the bestselling

00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:41.670
Anne Rice novel of the same name. But being
part of 1994's most eagerly anticipated beefcake

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:46.069
summit wasn't all it was cracked up to be
for Pitt. Years after the movie came and went,

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:50.170
Pitt admitted he was so miserable during filming
that he actually approached producer David

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:55.370
Geffen about buying out his contract—only
to reconsider after Geffen informed him it'd

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.340
cost $40 million.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:59.590
RUSSELL CROWE IN ROBIN HOOD

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:04.420
Usually, an actor who's cast to play a major
role in a movie can be on counted on to identify

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.760
with the basic idea of the story. For the
exception to that rule, we turn to Russell

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:13.620
Crowe, who agreed to star in Nottingham. However,
he thought the concept—a role-reversal on

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.130
the Robin Hood story, with the Sheriff of
Nottingham turning out to be the real good

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:22.660
guy—was fundamentally flawed. Demanding
a complete overhaul of the script, Crowe ended

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:27.830
up costing the studio millions in extra development,
and the result was the retitled Robin Hood,

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:32.750
one of 2010's most critically lambasted major
releases.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.000
EMMA WATSON IN HARRY POTTER AND THE ORDER
OF THE PHOENIX

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.240
For a lot of actors, scoring a regular role
in a successful franchise can mean the difference

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.930
between financial security and decades of
struggle. While Emma Watson definitely enjoyed

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:49.120
the career opportunities from her part in
the Harry Potter films, she gave some serious

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.740
thought to quitting. Watson reached her crossroads
during contract renegotiations for the fifth

00:04:54.740 --> 00:05:00.240
chapter, 2006's Order of the Phoenix, after
years of enduring strict studio oversight

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.590
over schedule and appearance. Although she
eventually re-signed, she admitted feeling

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:08.250
a major sense of relief after the series ended.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.370
AL PACINO IN THE GODFATHER

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:15.520
Al Pacino earned a Best Supporting Actor Oscar
nomination for his work as Michael Corleone

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.020
in The Godfather and a Best Actor nomination
for the sequel, so it's laughable to think

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:24.960
studio execs initially wanted him removed
from the role. Yet according to Pacino, that's

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.740
exactly what happened. He felt their resistance
so acutely, in fact, that he later claimed

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:34.190
he would have walked off the set if director
Francis Ford Coppola hadn't been so supportive

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.750
during those early days on the Godfather shoot.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.860
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:41.440
just saw.

